      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Percutaneous intervention in patients with intracoronary thrombus continues to pose a significant clinical challenge. In this report, we describe the successful treatment of a 44-year-old patient with an extensive chronic thrombotic occlusion of the right coronary artery using a rheolytic thrombectomy catheter. Despite angiographic documentation of coronary thrombosis 104 days prior to treatment and a voluminous thrombus burden (60 mm in length x 3 mm in diameter), rapid recanalization was accomplished with this device without embolic complications. At 1 year clinical follow-up, the patient has remained symptom free. The design of this novel device and its mechanism of action are described."},
